    <div class="case-grid" id="caseText">
      <div>
        <p>Liubomyr was a designer. Creative UI/UX, visual systems, motion. The kind of work
        where everything is supposed to have a reason. He took that part seriously.
        Perhaps too seriously.</p>
        <p>A shape was never just a shape. Type was never just type. Motion had a purpose,
        even when the purpose was simply to make you feel that something had shifted.</p>
        <p>He had little interest in the obvious answer. The ob9vious answer had usually been
        done before. He preferred the uncomfortable one. The one that made the room quieter.
        The one that made someone stop and look again.</p>
        <p>He understood something most people in his field eventually forgot.
        <b>Attention is not given. It is taken.</b></p>
        <p>Pretty things disappear quickly. They get approved. They get launched.
        They get replaced. He wanted something with a pulse. Something that could still
        say something after the novelty had worn off.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p>Photography came naturally after that. Or maybe it had been there all along.
        Liubomyr carried a camera and looked at the world through a frame — a shadow fell
        across a wall, a stranger disappeared around a corner, and a perfectly ordinary
        moment became impossible to explain.</p>
        <p>The ordinary was where things hid best. A kitchen at the wrong hour. A street
        after everyone had gone home. Nothing dramatic. Nothing worth mentioning.
        Until you looked at the photograph. Then it stayed with you.</p>
        <p>He had a problem with things that almost worked. Almost good. Almost finished.
        Almost original. A weak idea didn't become stronger because you spent another week
        polishing it.</p>
        <p>There was no grand philosophy behind any of it. No manifesto. Just an
        unreasonable amount of curiosity, and a refusal to leave things alone.</p>
        <p>Most people stop at the question. He didn't. He turned the question into
        something. A screen. A system. A photograph. A moment.</p>
